    {
      "id": "62",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 62,
        "body_ja": null,
        "body": "<p><span data-gp-id='48'>に行（い）く</span> is a grammar construction that is used when someone is going somewhere for the purpose of doing (A). (A) representing the verb that comes before <span data-gp-id='48'>に行（い）く</span>. As with other uses of <span data-gp-id='32'>に</span>, this particle is highlighting an end point or goal of 'going'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When using this structure, it will be important to remember that (A) will appear in its stem form, before <span data-gp-id='48'>に行（い）く</span> gets attached to the end.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154938'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154939'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>This grammar point is regularly translated as 'to go in order to (A)'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you have already 'gone' somewhere for the purpose of doing something, then you would use '(A) <span data-gp-id='32'>に</span><span data-gp-id='39'>来（き）た</span>', while at the location.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154940'></ul>"
      }
    },
